#. §0: `(S ∘ R).dom`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L04_Relations
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L06_Relations
msgid "You don't need to use unfold first to unpack the existential quantifier in §0; we could grab it directly too. The English analog of this is how we don't write the existential quantifier in ''indexed'' set builder notation."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `R`
#. §1: `R a b`
#. §2: `R`
#. §3: `R.set`
#. §4: `R a b`
#. §5: `(a,b) ∈ R`
#. §6: `(a,b) ∈ R.set`
#. §7: `Rel.inv`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L03_Relations
msgid "Relations in the Intro Proofs Game are defined with the ''aRb'' notation in mind, but this ''infix'' notation isn't used. If §0 is a relation from A to B, we'll write §1 to say that a relates to b.\n"
"Given a relation §2, we can access the corresponding set of ordered pairs by using §3. To be clear, if §4 is true, then we can't just write §5 - we will have to write §6.\n"
"To get used to this, let's prove something very basic - the inverse of a relation's inverse is the original relation.\n"
"Check your inventory for the exact definition of §7 so you can use it in your proof."
msgstr ""

